// TestDiscovery — every `test` declaration a project contains, read off the compiler's own
// DECLARATION SWEEP rather than off the source text.
//
// ⭐⭐ **DISCOVERY IS A PARSE, NOT A SEARCH, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE DESIGN.** A regex over `*.test.maxon`
// would find a `test` inside a comment or a string, would miss the file rule (E2058), and would have to
// re-derive the `__test_<sanitized>` mangling the compiler already performs — three ways of disagreeing
// with the binary that is about to be built. `queryProgramSignatures` already walks every file's tokens
// and already mints each test's registration name (`Project.testFunctionRegistrationName`), so this asks
// IT, and the roster a run prints is the roster the compile registers, by construction.
//
// ⭐ **IT STOPS AT THE SWEEP, WHICH IS WHAT LETS IT ANSWER FOR A PROJECT THAT HAS NO ENTRY POINT YET.**
// `maxon test --list` is documented as compiling nothing, and a project of pure test files declares no
// `main` — E3001 lives in `SemanticCheck`, far downstream of here, so nothing on this path can raise it.
// The bootstrap draws the identical line for the identical reason (`Compiler.DiscoverTests`: *"It runs
// CompileSources and NOTHING ELSE"*), one pass earlier because shv2's sweep is a token walk where the
// bootstrap's is a parse.

// ⭐⭐ **ONE `test` DECLARATION, AS THE SWEEP SAW IT.** A test has TWO names and this carries both, for
// `specs/test-declaration.md`'s reason: the prose name cannot be a symbol, so the compiler mints a
// sanitized one beside it, and neither is derivable from the other — sanitizing is lossy — so the pair
// travels together everywhere.
//
// ⚠ **`symbol` IS READ OFF THE MINT, NEVER RE-DERIVED FROM `displayName`.** They are produced by one
// call at one site (`Parser.recordScannedTest`), which is what makes a generated dispatcher's call
// resolve: a second application of the sanitizing rule here would be a second thing to get wrong, and it
// would fail as a call to a function that does not exist rather than as a mismatch anybody can see.
export type DiscoveredTest
	// The mangled name the test's function actually has — `__test_<sanitized>`. What generated source
	// calls, and what reaches the executable's symbol table.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS BARE, WHERE THE BOOTSTRAP'S IS NAMESPACE-QUALIFIED, AND THAT IS shv2's RULE RATHER THAN AN
	// OMISSION.** The bootstrap qualifies every free function by its directory, so its `DiscoveredTest`
	// has to carry a `Namespace`/`ShortSymbol` split to recover the callable spelling. shv2 registers a
	// free function under exactly the bytes it was declared with UNLESS the bare name is declared in two
	// directories (`ProgramSignatures.contestedFreeFunctions`, N1c) — and a minted `__test_` name cannot
	// be contested by a name any author can write (E2051 refuses the prefix). So the symbol IS the
	// callable spelling, from any file, and a split would be a rule shv2 does not have.
	export let symbol as ByteArray
	// The prose name, verbatim — what a report shows a human, and what `test '…'` wrote.
	export let displayName as String
	// The `*.test.maxon` file holding the declaration, as the compiler recorded it. A report groups and
	// orders by this, so it is identity and not decoration.
	export let file as FilePath
	// The line the declaration's name sits on.
	export let line as LineNumber

	export static function create(symbol ByteArray, displayName String, file FilePath, line LineNumber) returns DiscoveredTest
		return Self{symbol: symbol, displayName: displayName, file: file, line: line}
	end 'create'
end 'DiscoveredTest'

// Discovered tests IN THE ORDER THE SWEEP REACHED THEM, which is the walk's file order and, within a
// file, declaration order. The ORDER is the fact this alias carries: it is the run's numbering, the
// report's reading order, and what makes a redirected run byte-reproducible.
export typealias DiscoveredTestArray = Array with DiscoveredTest

// ⭐⭐ **EVERY `test` UNDER `projectDir`.** Loads the project WITH its test files (the one selection an
// ordinary build does not make — see `Compiler.SourceSelection`) and runs the declaration sweep over it.
//
// ⚠ **THE TARGET IS A PARAMETER AND IS NOT DEFAULTED TO THE HOST**, which is the bootstrap's measured
// rule (`Compiler.DiscoverTests`): the sweep resolves `#if os(…)` / `arch(…)`, so a test behind one
// exists for some targets and not others. Discovering for the host and building for `--target` would
// drop a test from the report without saying so, and emit a dispatcher call to one the real compile
// cannot resolve.
//
// The tests are COPIED out of the index rather than handed back as a borrow of it: the index is a memo
// owned by a `Project` this function is the last holder of, and a roster that outlived its project would
// be a view into freed storage.
export function discoverTests(projectDir FilePath, target Target) returns DiscoveredTestArray throws CompileError
	var project = try loadProject([projectDir], target: target, sourceSelection: SourceSelection.productionAndTests)
	let signatures = try queryProgramSignatures(project)

	var found = DiscoveredTestArray.create()
	for declared in signatures.declaredTests 'eachDeclaredTest'
		found.push(DiscoveredTest.create(declared.symbol.clone(), displayName: declared.displayName, file: declared.file, line: declared.line))
	end 'eachDeclaredTest'

	return found
end 'discoverTests'
